version-declared.remediation:
Declare the PHP versions the package supports in `composer.json`:

```json
{
  "require": { "php": ">=8.2" }
}
```

If you know the deployed runtime, add `"config": { "platform": { "php": "8.2.18" } }` next to it, because that is the version Composer resolves against. Without either, `composer update` resolves against whichever PHP the machine running it has. A package that needs a newer runtime than production installs without complaint, and the failure shows up on the first request instead of at install time.

dependencies-constrained.remediation:
Give every requirement a constraint naming the major release it was written against:

```json
{
  "require": {
    "symfony/console": "^7.0"
  }
}
```

A `*` requirement takes whatever the registry serves, including the next major release, so an upstream release can change what this project installs without any change in this repository.
